---
// Sources: Lunaaaalj/Stochastic_RefInit: PRODUCT.md (decisions, users, site), reports/e2
// (purchasing model, spatial model, results), data/processed (species, competition matrix,
// polygon map). The exact-model comparison is pending in the report and is reported as such.
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import TeX from '../components/TeX.astro';
import MosaicFigure from '../components/MosaicFigure.astro';
---
<Project
  slug="reforestation"
  abstract="Two decisions for CONAFOR’s reforestation of 30 polygons along the Dominica–Charcas transmission line in San Luis Potosí: how many plants of each of ten native species to buy so that every polygon meets its planting target with probability at least 1 − α, and how to arrange the species on the planting grid so that neighbouring plants compete as little as possible. At the budget of MXN 12,500 per hectare no polygon is feasible; the minimum is MXN 14,496 per hectare."
>
  <section class="fig-section" aria-label="Figure 1">
    <MosaicFigure />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="problem-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="problem-h">Problem</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The site is 30 polygons, from 1.28 to 8 ha and 183.01 ha in all, along the Dominica–Charcas power
          transmission line in San Luis Potosí. The plan calls for 658 plants per hectare of ten native species,
          four agaves, four prickly pears, mesquite and yucca, set out on a staggered grid.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two decisions follow. The first is how many plants of each species to buy for each polygon. It is made
          without knowing how many plants are already established in each polygon, or how many of the new ones will
          survive their first year. The second is where each plant goes, so that neighbours compete as little as
          possible for water and space. The project’s desktop tool, <code>refor_gui</code>, is designed for CONAFOR’s
          planning technicians to run themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Buy too few and the polygon falls short of its target once plants die; buy too many and the budget runs out. The question is how many to buy so that falling short is unlikely.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="buy-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="buy-h">Formulation: purchasing</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          For polygon <TeX tex="i" /> of area <TeX tex="A_i" /> and species <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex="s" />,</span> the plants already
          present are <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`E_{is} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_s\, A_i)`} />,</span> and the survivors
          of <TeX tex="x" /> new plants are <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`S_{is}(x) \sim \operatorname{Bin}(x, q_s)`} />.</span> The
          decisions are the target <TeX tex={String.raw`n_{is}`} /> and the purchase <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`x_{is}`} />,</span> both integer.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Existing plants are scattered at random over each polygon; each new plant survives its first year independently with its species’ observed probability.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Objective</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\min_{n,\,x}\; (1+\theta)\sum_s c_s x_{is} + \sum_s c^R_s\, \mathbb{E}\!\left[D_{is}\right]`} /><span class="eqn">(1)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The cost of the plants, with logistics, plus the expected cost of replacing those that die. <TeX tex={String.raw`D_{is}`} /> is the shortfall after the first year.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Constraints</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\sum_s n_{is} = 658\,A_i, \qquad \lvert n_{is} - n^0_s\, A_i \rvert \le \delta\, n^0_s\, A_i`} /><span class="eqn">(2)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`(1+\theta)\sum_s c_s x_{is} \le B\,A_i`} /><span class="eqn">(3)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\mathbb{P}\!\left(E_{is} + S_{is}(x_{is}) \ge n_{is}\right) \ge 1 - \alpha`} /><span class="eqn">(4)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>The total density is fixed, and each species may move at most 10% from the plan. Spending stays within the budget per hectare.</p>
        <p>The chance constraint: each species reaches its target with probability at least 95% (<span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha = 0.05`} />)</span>.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="space-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="space-h">Formulation: arrangement</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The ground is a graph whose vertices are planting positions and whose edges join neighbours. A symmetric
          matrix <TeX tex="W" /> gives the penalty for each pair of species side by side; the arrangement minimises the
          total penalty over neighbouring pairs, respecting the number of plants of each species.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Competition matrix</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`W_{ab} = \gamma^{\,\mathbf{1}[a=b]}\; T_{ab}\, N_{ab} - \phi\, F_{ab}`} /><span class="eqn">(5)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p><span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`T_{ab}`} />:</span> how much of the spacing the two adult canopies cover. <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`N_{ab}`} />:</span> how much their roots draw on the same soil layer. <TeX tex={String.raw`\gamma = 1.5`} /> makes two plants of the same species compete more; <TeX tex={String.raw`\phi = 0.3`} /> credits mesquite as a nurse plant for the succulents.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          Plant-by-plant coordinates, which pair-correlation methods need, are not in the data, and the counts per
          polygon carry no signal about interactions between neighbours (only 4 of 45 species-pair correlations are
          significant at 5%). The matrix is therefore built from species traits, and its robustness is checked with
          400 alternative matrices, varying each canopy diameter by ±30%, <TeX tex={String.raw`\gamma`} /> in
          [1, 2] and <TeX tex={String.raw`\phi`} /> in [0, 0.6]; the order of the entries holds (mean Spearman
          correlation 0.93).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="method-h">Method</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          Species are coupled only through the total density and the budget, so purchasing is solved in two stages. For
          each species and each candidate target, 10,000 Monte Carlo scenarios estimate the probability of meeting it;
          because that probability does not decrease with the purchase, the minimum purchase that reaches <TeX tex={String.raw`1-\alpha`} /> is found by bisection, with common random numbers across candidates. Choosing
          one target per species is then a multiple-choice knapsack, solved exactly. The procedure is a sample average
          approximation; 10,000 scenarios give a 95% interval of half-width 0.005 around a probability of 0.95.
        </p>
        <p>
          The arrangement is optimised on a tile of 52 positions with periodic borders, which a planting crew repeats over
          the polygon as a table of species codes. A memetic algorithm combines a genetic algorithm, with a crossover
          adapted from GPX that preserves each species’ count, and tabu search over swaps of two positions. An integer
          program with 520 binary variables, solved with HiGHS, is set up to bound the memetic algorithm’s distance from
          the optimum. Everything is written in C++20 and is reproducible: the same seed gives the same result on any
          platform and any number of threads.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Simulation answers “how likely is this purchase to be enough?”; the knapsack picks the cheapest combination of species that keeps every answer at 95% or better.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="results-h">Results</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          With MXN 12,500 per hectare, no polygon is feasible. The minimum budget that satisfies every constraint is, on
          average weighted by area, MXN 14,496 per hectare (from 14,361 to 14,934), 16% more than is available. The
          shortfall is structural and is not closed by accepting more risk: the minimum budget falls only from MXN 14,664
          at <TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha = 0.01`} /> to 14,117 at <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha = 0.50`} />,</span> under 4% for a
          fiftyfold increase in the probability of falling short.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without the budget constraint, the purchase is 108,680 plants for MXN 2.65 million, 594 plants per hectare.
          With the 129 per hectare already established, planting density is 723 per hectare, 10% over target: the
          overplanting that absorbs first-year mortality at the required reliability. The composition moves to the edge
          of the 10% band in the direction of cost per surviving plant: the four prickly pears rise 10%, mesquite, yucca
          and <i>A. scabra</i> fall 10%.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rounded to 52 positions, the 30 polygons reduce to six distinct tiles. The memetic algorithm reduces
          competition by 14.5% to 16.3% against a random arrangement of the same composition, with a standard deviation
          over 30 runs of at most 0.006. Same-species neighbours disappear except for one or two pairs of
          <i>A. salmiana</i>, which fills 27–29% of positions, out of 156 neighbouring pairs; mesquite never sits next to
          its own species and acts as a separator.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>The binding cost is the shortfall itself, the plants needed to reach 658 per hectare, not the safety margin. That is why accepting more risk saves so little.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <table class="data">
          <caption class="vh">Planting tiles of 52 positions and the memetic algorithm's results.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tile</th><th scope="col" class="n">Polygons</th><th scope="col" class="n">Best competition</th><th scope="col" class="n">Mean ± s.d., 30 runs</th><th scope="col" class="n">Coefficient</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td class="n">18</td><td class="n">78.18</td><td class="n">78.19 ± 0.01</td><td class="n">0.838</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td class="n">5</td><td class="n">77.37</td><td class="n">77.39 ± 0.01</td><td class="n">0.838</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td class="n">1</td><td class="n">81.29</td><td class="n">81.29 ± 0.00</td><td class="n">0.855</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td class="n">1</td><td class="n">76.77</td><td class="n">76.78 ± 0.01</td><td class="n">0.837</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td class="n">4</td><td class="n">76.80</td><td class="n">76.80 ± 0.00</td><td class="n">0.837</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">6</th><td class="n">1</td><td class="n">81.85</td><td class="n">81.85 ± 0.00</td><td class="n">0.853</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">From the second report. The coefficient is competition divided by its expected value under a random arrangement.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The comparison with the exact integer program, its lower bound and the memetic algorithm’s gap, is still pending in the report.</p></aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>

<style>
  .fig-section { margin-top: 56px; }
  .table-note { margin: 8px 0 0; font: 400 14px/1.45 var(--sans); color: var(--graphite); }
  code { font-size: 0.85em; }
</style>
